<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muʻa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mu'a, Lapaha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muʻa is a town on the shore of the lagoon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongatapu">Tongatapu</a>, the main island of Tonga. From about the 12th or 13th century its district of Lapaha was the seat of the Tuʻi Tonga, the sacred kings, and the centre of a chiefdom whose influence reached across western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. It holds the langi, large stepped royal tombs faced with cut limestone slabs, which are among the most important monuments of the Pacific.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mua-tonga.jpg" alt="Muʻa" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Langi tauʻolunga / Tauʻolunga · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mu%27a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
